  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms are \npossible across portions of eastern Pennsylvania and New Jersey \ninto tonight. Temperatures will be warmer with increasing \nhumidity as well.\n\nThis afternoon, the high pressure that has been dominating our \nweather for the past day has shifted off the Mid-Atlantic coast,\nand will continue to slide eastward with time. Warm and moist \nsouthwesterly flow has commenced across the region, with \ntemperatures rising into the mid 70s to upper 80s from north to \nsouth. Widespread cloud cover over the northern half of the area\nwill continue to limit heating this afternoon, but temperatures\nmay rise a couple more degrees into late this afternoon across \nsouthern NJ and Delmarva.\n\nThe atmosphere across portions of southeastern PA and southern \nNJ is modestly destabilizing, with Objective Analysis indicating\naround 500 J/kg of MLCAPE. Some additional destabilization is \nlikely through the afternoon, with at least pockets of 1000 J/kg\nexpected. With modest forcing overspreading the region, \nisolated to scattered showers are developing across portions of \nsoutheastern PA. Thus far, this activity has mostly struggled. \nHowever, some increase in storm coverage and intensity is \nexpected as we head toward the evening hours. Locally heavy \nrainfall is possible with the strongest storms. Some localized \nflooding can't be ruled out especially in areas that have \nreceived substantial rainfall recently. However, overall this \npotential is expected to remain fairly limited. WPC has placed \neastern PA and NJ under a Marginal Risk (1 of 4) for excessive \nrainfall today. The threat for severe thunderstorms is low, \nthough a few instances of gusty winds or small hail will be \npossible with the strongest storms. \n\nScattered showers may persist well into the overnight hours as \nforcing remains aloft.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Heat and humidity will build into the middle of\nthe week, along with daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. There is an increasing threat of severe \nthunderstorms and potentially flash flooding during the middle \nof the week, namely Tuesday through Thursday.\n\nBroad upper level ridging returns to the eastern US early next \nweek, bringing in surface high pressure across the eastern US. \nAfternoon highs will creep back into the mid 80s to low 90s for \nmost areas, about 5 degrees above average for early September. \nDewpoints will also remain in the upper 60s to low 70s. The \nhottest day looks to be Tuesday, when high temperatures could \nget into the mid 90s across Delmarva and into the Philly metro. \nResulting heat indices will be well into the 90s to around 100. \nWe will continue to keep an eye on the potential for Heat \nAdvisories.\n\nOne other aspect to watch with this ridging pattern is the \npotential for thunderstorms to rotate around the periphery of \nthe ridge and the position of a frontal boundary during the \nmiddle of the week. Latest guidance suggests that Monday may be \nmostly dry, though isolated storms can't be ruled out. Guidance \ncontinues to come into consensus that the pattern will be \nsupportive of multiple rounds of thunderstorms, especially \nduring the Tuesday through Thursday time frame.\n\nSPC has placed portions of the area in a Slight Risk (2 of 5) \nfor severe thunderstorms Tuesday. Wednesday and Thursday are \nalso highlighted with 15% probabilities for severe \nthunderstorms. The northwest flow pattern that will be in place \nwill be supportive of all severe threats, including damaging \nwinds, large hail, and tornadoes. The threat for flash flooding \nis typically lower with these patterns, however given multiple \ndays with the potential for storms and recent heavy rainfalls, \nat least some risk for flash flooding will exist. Long story \nshort, the Tuesday through Thursday period looks very active \nwith multiple rounds of thunderstorms across the region, but \ndetails remain unclear. Stay tuned for updates on these threats \nover the coming days.\n\nBy Friday, current model consensus indicates the frontal \nboundary will be pushing south of our region with Canadian high \npressure building across the Northeast. This should favor an end\nto severe weather and flash flooding potential.",
